{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016pzgaGScU5ec8yYxKk82xt","type":"tool_result","content":"20\t  `PICOQUIC_TEST_LIBRARY_FILES` list).  Single target\n21\t  (`x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`).\n22\t* `no_std` + `alloc`, with an `std` feature gate.\n23\t\n24\tOut of scope for v1 (explicit follow-on work):\n25\t\n26\t* `loglib/` (the `picoquic-log` target) and `picohttp/` (the\n27\t  `picohttp-core` HTTP/3 target).  These layer on top of picoquic-core\n28\t  and can be ported in a v2 expansion.\n29\t\n30\t* Multi-threading.  v1 assumes single-threaded C; pthread / atomics /\n31\t  volatile usage in the C source becomes a translation note (\"threading\n32\t  dropped, revisit in v2\"), not an in-line decision.\n33\t* Multi-platform support.  Code that's purely `#ifdef _WIN32` (or any\n34\t  non-target branch) is not translated in v1; other platforms get added\n35\t  back later behind Rust `#[cfg(target_os = …)]`.\n36\t* Differential testing against the C library.\n37\t* Performance benchmarking, fuzzing, mutation testing, coverage gating.\n38\t* Stable ABI / FFI compatibility with the existing C library.  This is a\n39\t  hard fork — there is no incremental cutover, no shim, no mixed build.\n40\t\n41\t## Guiding rules (apply throughout)\n42\t\n43\t1. **Safety always wins.**  When safe-idiomatic Rust and bug-for-bug\n44\t   compatibility conflict (integer overflow, signed shifts, overlapping\n45\t   `memcpy`, uninitialized reads, pointer provenance), choose the safe\n46\t   idiom even if outputs diverge for pathological inputs.\n47\t2. **Mirror the C source line-by-line.**  Same control flow, same variable\n48\t   lifetimes, same allocation pattern.  The Rust function should look like\n49\t   a translation, not a reinterpretation.\n50\t3. **Bug-for-bug compatibility is the default behavior**, achieved by\n51\t   source-level mimicry.  Empirical equivalence with the C library will be\n52\t   verified later, in a follow-on phase, by a binary that links both\n53\t   libraries and diffs outputs on shared inputs.\n54\t\n55\t## Cross-cutting policies\n56\t\n57\t### Memory and ownership\n58\t\n59\t* Default to `Box`, owned values, and borrowing.  Follow the C ownership\n60\t  model where it makes sense.\n61\t* `Rc<RefCell<T>>` is the last-resort escape hatch, used only when the\n62\t  ownership graph genuinely isn't a tree (mutual references, callbacks\n63\t  with shared mutable state).\n64\t* `Send` and `Sync` are not required in v1 (single-threaded scope).  v2\n65\t  multi-threading will revisit ownership comprehensively.\n66\t\n67\t### Pointer translation (Phase 1 rules)\n68\t\n69\t* Pointer fields and parameters get a per-case Rust shape decided by"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"621e8ef5-6ea2-41f7-92c8-884ed614789d","uuid":"47205e39-ec14-4f09-aacc-78737aff2fc6","timestamp":"2026-05-06T12:22:40.988Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/TRANSLATE_PLAN.md","content":"  `PICOQUIC_TEST_LIBRARY_FILES` list).  Single target\n  (`x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`).\n* `no_std` + `alloc`, with an `std` feature gate.\n\nOut of scope for v1 (explicit follow-on work):\n\n* `loglib/` (the `picoquic-log` target) and `picohttp/` (the\n  `picohttp-core` HTTP/3 target).  These layer on top of picoquic-core\n  and can be ported in a v2 expansion.\n\n* Multi-threading.  v1 assumes single-threaded C; pthread / atomics /\n  volatile usage in the C source becomes a translation note (\"threading\n  dropped, revisit in v2\"), not an in-line decision.\n* Multi-platform support.  Code that's purely `#ifdef _WIN32` (or any\n  non-target branch) is not translated in v1; other platforms get added\n  back later behind Rust `#[cfg(target_os = …)]`.\n* Differential testing against the C library.\n* Performance benchmarking, fuzzing, mutation testing, coverage gating.\n* Stable ABI / FFI compatibility with the existing C library.  This is a\n  hard fork — there is no incremental cutover, no shim, no mixed build.\n\n## Guiding rules (apply throughout)\n\n1. **Safety always wins.**  When safe-idiomatic Rust and bug-for-bug\n   compatibility conflict (integer overflow, signed shifts, overlapping\n   `memcpy`, uninitialized reads, pointer provenance), choose the safe\n   idiom even if outputs diverge for pathological inputs.\n2. **Mirror the C source line-by-line.**  Same control flow, same variable\n   lifetimes, same allocation pattern.  The Rust function should look like\n   a translation, not a reinterpretation.\n3. **Bug-for-bug compatibility is the default behavior**, achieved by\n   source-level mimicry.  Empirical equivalence with the C library will be\n   verified later, in a follow-on phase, by a binary that links both\n   libraries and diffs outputs on shared inputs.\n\n## Cross-cutting policies\n\n### Memory and ownership\n\n* Default to `Box`, owned values, and borrowing.  Follow the C ownership\n  model where it makes sense.\n* `Rc<RefCell<T>>` is the last-resort escape hatch, used only when the\n  ownership graph genuinely isn't a tree (mutual references, callbacks\n  with shared mutable state).\n* `Send` and `Sync` are not required in v1 (single-threaded scope).  v2\n  multi-threading will revisit ownership comprehensively.\n\n### Pointer translation (Phase 1 rules)\n\n* Pointer fields and parameters get a per-case Rust shape decided by","numLines":50,"startLine":20,"totalLines":1038}}}
